1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
It allows you to effortlessly control your car

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
and provides precision maneuvering for the most advanced military aircraft.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
But this workhorse of industry is as basic as fluids in motion.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Now, Engineering's Hidden Marvel, Hydraulics, on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,840
Our industrialized world depends on hydraulics.

6
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:41,280
But hydraulics is so fundamental to our way of life that we hardly notice it.

7
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:49,260
Hydraulics has taken us from the Model T to the SUV, from Kitty Hawk to the F-22 Raptor.

8
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:57,440
Hydraulics provides the muscles that lift, steer, and drive excavators that mine Earth's precious resources.

9
00:00:57,441 --> 00:01:04,100
and is the force behind the machinery that fabricates the very structure of our cities.

10
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:08,080
Hydraulics elevates us, thrills us.

11
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:12,540
It even picks up our garbage.

12
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:16,320
You look at a lot of applications and we see hydraulics.

13
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:20,440
If you think about a car going down the street, of course you have hydraulic power steering, hydraulic brakes.

14
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:24,820
A lot of the roofs on those cars, the convertible tops are all hydraulic controlled.

15
00:01:24,821 --> 00:01:27,820
Get into aircraft, all the control surfaces.

16
00:01:27,821 --> 00:01:30,820
Amusement parks, a lot of the rides today,

17
00:01:30,821 --> 00:01:32,820
there's hydraulic powered roller coasters.

18
00:01:32,821 --> 00:01:34,820
Oh my God!

19
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:42,820
Go 0 to 120 in 4 seconds.

20
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:48,820
Hydraulics is the branch of physics that studies the behavior

21
00:01:48,821 --> 00:01:51,820
of liquids at rest or in motion.

22
00:01:51,821 --> 00:01:58,820
liquid in motion. Liquid in motion is referred to as hydrodynamics.

23
00:01:58,821 --> 00:02:02,820
Civil engineers use this form of hydraulics in hydroelectric power plants,

24
00:02:02,821 --> 00:02:07,820
manipulating the flow of liquid to push turbines. Those power plants provide

25
00:02:07,821 --> 00:02:13,820
electricity to over a billion people worldwide. The second type of hydraulics,

26
00:02:13,821 --> 00:02:19,820
liquids at rest, is referred to as hydrostatics. Mechanical engineers use

27
00:02:19,821 --> 00:02:23,860
mechanical engineers use this form of hydraulics to produce force through the pressurization

28
00:02:23,861 --> 00:02:30,860
of incompressible liquids. If you have two pistons within a closed hydraulic system,

29
00:02:30,861 --> 00:02:36,860
any force that is applied to piston A will be completely transferred to piston B.

30
00:02:36,861 --> 00:02:44,260
The power of your blow is transferred to the other plunger by hydraulic transfer of power.

31
00:02:44,261 --> 00:02:48,920
One of the benefits of a closed hydraulic system is the ability to multiply force.

32
00:02:50,620 --> 00:03:01,920
If piston B has a surface area 10 times that of piston A, piston B will move one unit for every 10 units that piston A moves, but at 10 times the force.

33
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:12,080
Therefore, 100 pounds of force applied to piston A will supply enough pressure under piston B to move an object weighing 1,000 pounds.

34
00:03:12,081 --> 00:03:18,880
Remarkable in its simplicity, hydraulic multiplication of force is awesome in its capability

35
00:03:18,881 --> 00:03:23,060
and is what delivers the brute force for nearly every large machine.

36
00:03:24,540 --> 00:03:30,200
In most people's mind, they consider hydraulics the application of high-pressure fluids,

37
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:35,160
generally speaking, to move large amounts of weight and generate a lot of force.

38
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:42,000
Some of the more impressive applications of hydraulics are just pure raw hydraulic force.

39
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:47,580
You look at car crushers as an example.

40
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:48,420
It's unbelievable.

41
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:53,080
You'll take five or six cars piled vertically and they're smashed to just feet.

42
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:55,280
And you have to say, how is that done?

43
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:56,620
It's hydraulics.

44
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:05,260
One of the most common devices that utilizes hydraulic multiplication of force is the hydraulic jack.

45
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:10,560
When you push the lever up and down, you're actually pushing on a small piston,

46
00:04:10,561 --> 00:04:15,560
which pushes hydraulic fluid, and this fluid flows into a larger piston.

47
00:04:15,740 --> 00:04:19,820
Say the small piston that you're pushing has a surface area of one square inch,

48
00:04:19,821 --> 00:04:23,260
but the large piston that's actually pushing up your car,

49
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:27,300
that might have a surface area of, say, ten square inches.

50
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:30,160
So for every pound of force you push with that lever,

51
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:33,100
the hydraulic system multiplies that force ten times.

52
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:38,000
Unfortunately, in hydraulics, like in everything else, there's no free lunch.

53
00:04:38,001 --> 00:04:41,440
so you don't get it for free.

54
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:43,640
In order to get that to work,

55
00:04:43,780 --> 00:04:46,560
you have to pump 10 times the amount of fluid.

56
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:50,820
Although the basic principles behind hydraulics may appear simple,

57
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:53,980
it took many brilliant minds thousands of years

58
00:04:53,981 --> 00:04:56,660
to fully understand the inherent power of fluid.

59
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:01,540
The Greeks in 250 B.C.

60
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:03,640
were one of the earliest societies

61
00:05:03,641 --> 00:05:05,920
to look for ways of controlling the flow of water,

62
00:05:05,921 --> 00:05:08,680
inventing many hydraulic devices.

63
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,480
Some were devised for amusement,

64
00:05:12,980 --> 00:05:16,000
like this invention that forced air out of a small bent tube,

65
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:17,660
producing the sound of a bird.

66
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:21,460
Others were more useful, such as the water clock.

67
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:26,060
The Greek who made the most lasting contribution to hydraulics

68
00:05:26,061 --> 00:05:27,900
was the mathematician Archimedes,

69
00:05:27,901 --> 00:05:31,880
who discovered the first principle of hydrostatics,

70
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:33,600
buoyancy.

71
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:38,800
His buoyancy experiment was basically based on the concept

72
00:05:38,801 --> 00:05:41,620
that a material that is submerged to water

73
00:05:41,621 --> 00:05:45,060
experiences a force against gravity,

74
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:48,300
and that force is known as pressure.

75
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:50,780
He also was the same inventor who said,

76
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:52,900
if you give me a lever long enough in a place to stand,

77
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:53,780
I can move the earth.

78
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:56,000
Fundamentally, that's the principle of levers,

79
00:05:56,540 --> 00:05:58,660
and realistically, that's what we're doing with hydraulics

80
00:05:58,661 --> 00:05:59,580
on a daily basis.

81
00:05:59,581 --> 00:06:05,580
By 30 BC, the Roman Empire had cast its shadow across the borders of Greece.

82
00:06:05,581 --> 00:06:11,580
But instead of destroying Greek society, the Romans learned from it, improving upon several

83
00:06:11,581 --> 00:06:16,580
Greek inventions, including the water wheel and the aqueduct.

84
00:06:16,581 --> 00:06:22,580
The Romans, they realized that they had to feed bigger areas, then they had to have a

85
00:06:22,581 --> 00:06:28,580
system where they can carry large quantities and at the same time use the concept of gravity.

86
00:06:28,581 --> 00:06:31,140
concept of gravity.

87
00:06:31,141 --> 00:06:36,880
In the aqueducts, water flowed swiftly, producing as much as 1,300 pounds of pressure per square

88
00:06:36,881 --> 00:06:41,534
inch, 25 times the standard water pressure of a city today.

89
00:06:41,535 --> 00:06:43,900
Roman engineers often designed

90
00:06:43,901 --> 00:06:49,720
the aqueducts to end its cisterns, which would push water up through small openings, turning

91
00:06:49,721 --> 00:06:55,356
the liquid into a fountain and providing drinking water for the citizens of Rome.

92
00:06:55,357 --> 00:06:56,400
Other aqueducts

93
00:06:56,401 --> 00:06:59,640
were sent to water mills, transferring the water's energy

94
00:06:59,641 --> 00:07:03,760
into kinetic energy to grind grain into flour.

95
00:07:03,761 --> 00:07:06,320
But Roman engineers didn't develop further hydraulic

96
00:07:06,321 --> 00:07:12,020
technology, relying instead on the strength of slave labor.

97
00:07:12,021 --> 00:07:14,960
In the millennium that followed, the science of hydraulics

98
00:07:14,961 --> 00:07:16,940
didn't advance.

99
00:07:16,941 --> 00:07:18,900
Old technologies such as water mills

100
00:07:18,901 --> 00:07:20,560
flourished during medieval times.

101
00:07:20,561 --> 00:07:29,240
Finally, in the 17th century, French mathematician Blaise Pascal made a groundbreaking discovery.

102
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:36,380
By multiplying fluid depth by its inherent weight, Pascal created the formula to determine fluid pressure.

103
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:42,600
This led to the most important aspect of hydrostatics, multiplication of force.

104
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:48,660
Pascal's principle is the basis for all the work that we do in hydraulics today.

105
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:55,800
By 1795, Englishman Joseph Brahma had put Pascal's principle to work with his hydraulic press.

106
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:04,220
When the first hydraulic systems were used around the 1800s, they did use water as the hydraulic fluid.

107
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:06,660
And water does not make a very good hydraulic fluid.

108
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:10,400
Some of the problems it has, it's very thin, so it leaks past your seals.

109
00:08:10,620 --> 00:08:14,700
Back in the 1800s, the seals they had were things like leather and ropes and things of that sort.

110
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:17,640
So if they did seal, they didn't seal for very long.

111
00:08:18,660 --> 00:08:24,260
The inefficient hydraulic industry languished, but soon after the Brahma Press, a new discovery

112
00:08:24,261 --> 00:08:29,060
in America would lead indirectly to a new hydraulic technology.

113
00:08:29,061 --> 00:08:34,420
The discovery of gold at Sutter's Mill in 1848 led to one of the largest human migrations

114
00:08:34,421 --> 00:08:37,280
in history.

115
00:08:37,281 --> 00:08:42,420
Desiring a faster, quicker way to uncover riches, a new method of gold mining quickly

116
00:08:42,421 --> 00:08:46,420
spread throughout America, called hydraulicing.

117
00:08:46,421 --> 00:08:50,940
And for the next 50 years, hydraulic mining would literally change the landscape of America.

118
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:58,980
People have recognized for a long time that the power behind water can be enormous.

119
00:08:59,460 --> 00:09:03,840
And in the early mining days in the U.S., the gold rush era, we took major advantage of this.

120
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:07,120
Accessing water sources at much higher elevations,

121
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:11,240
miners constructed wood pipes and attached the ends to nozzles,

122
00:09:11,241 --> 00:09:17,320
creating hoses that could shoot water at high velocity against the California hillsides.

123
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:20,820
The hillsides disintegrated, releasing the gold.

124
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:25,740
The scientific principle behind this effective method of gold mining

125
00:09:25,741 --> 00:09:30,280
was established by Swiss mathematician Daniel Bernoulli in 1738.

126
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:35,280
Bernoulli found that if a containment of water is forced through a smaller opening,

127
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:39,060
such as a nozzle, the pressure that builds behind the opening

128
00:09:39,061 --> 00:09:42,020
would increase the water's velocity, creating a jet.

129
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:47,320
Although it's unlikely that the miners were aware of Bernoulli's principle,

130
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:49,760
their method of mining was effective nonetheless.

131
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:54,540
The pressures were on the order of 800 pounds per square inch to 1,000 pounds per square inch.

132
00:09:54,980 --> 00:09:57,600
So 1,000 pounds per square inch blasting against raw earth.

133
00:09:58,080 --> 00:09:59,640
You clean the soil away in a hurry.

134
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:04,040
Hydraulic nozzles called monitors reached 16 feet in length

135
00:10:04,041 --> 00:10:08,900
and could deliver as much as 30,000 gallons of water a minute in a 9-inch stream.

136
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:15,940
At its peak, the hydraulic mining industry in California employed 20,000 people and had

137
00:10:15,941 --> 00:10:20,820
returns close to $100 million in gold.

138
00:10:20,821 --> 00:10:26,500
The success of hydraulic gold mining was matched only by its devastating effect on the environment.

139
00:10:26,501 --> 00:10:31,680
By 1884, it was outlawed in California by a federal injunction.

140
00:10:31,681 --> 00:10:37,720
Although hydraulic gold mining had come to an end, a new kind of gold, black gold, was

141
00:10:37,721 --> 00:10:42,720
was about to bring hydraulics to the forefront of the Industrial Revolution.

142
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:48,720
Daniel Bernoulli's father, Johann Bernoulli, became so jealous

143
00:10:48,721 --> 00:10:52,720
after his son published his book, Hydrodynamica, in 1738,

144
00:10:52,721 --> 00:10:55,720
that he wrote his own book on hydraulics,

145
00:10:55,721 --> 00:11:00,720
and predated it in a futile attempt to take credit for his son's findings.

146
00:11:00,721 --> 00:11:12,160
Until the 19th century, hydraulic power was achieved primarily through harnessing the energy of flowing water.

147
00:11:13,740 --> 00:11:17,840
This would change in 1859 with the discovery of oil.

148
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:27,680
Unfortunately, water has this tendency to turn into solids at something like 32 degrees Fahrenheit.

149
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:30,160
That is, it freezes, turns into ice.

150
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:38,480
And it tends to boil at about 212, so it tends to have a relatively narrow operating window.

151
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:45,660
Oil, on the other hand, can have an operating temperature of well below zero degrees Fahrenheit

152
00:11:45,661 --> 00:11:48,800
to well above 250 degrees Fahrenheit.

153
00:11:49,580 --> 00:11:55,860
The discovery of a suitable hydraulic fluid made the hydraulic press an essential tool on the factory floor

154
00:11:55,861 --> 00:12:00,760
and helped to drive the evolution of America's newest obsession, the automobile.

155
00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:10,500
Hydraulics played a crucial part in enabling vehicles to transform from sort of the hay wagon type of thing

156
00:12:10,501 --> 00:12:16,520
to the ever heavier, ever more comfortable, ever more, you know, livable vehicles that we have today.

157
00:12:18,380 --> 00:12:25,180
By the early 1900s, the automobile industry began to provide a faster means of travel than the horse and carriage.

158
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:27,820
But this posed a difficult problem.

159
00:12:28,380 --> 00:12:30,140
How do you make the motorized carriage stop?

160
00:12:31,180 --> 00:12:32,394
In 1904, Ransom E.

161
00:12:32,395 --> 00:12:36,980
Olds designed the first widely used brake system for the automobile.

162
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:42,820
This external brake consisted of a single flexible stainless steel band

163
00:12:42,821 --> 00:12:45,720
wrapped around a drum on the rear axle of the car wheel.

164
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:49,940
When the brake pedal was applied, a cable would contract the band,

165
00:12:50,460 --> 00:12:52,340
gripping the drum and stopping the wheel.

166
00:12:52,341 --> 00:12:56,120
there was a lot of room for improvement

167
00:12:56,121 --> 00:12:59,820
one of the major flaws of the external brake

168
00:12:59,821 --> 00:13:02,640
was that it had no protection from outside elements

169
00:13:02,641 --> 00:13:05,140
most roads were unpaved

170
00:13:05,141 --> 00:13:07,860
and the metal bands and drums quickly wore down

171
00:13:07,861 --> 00:13:12,020
the external brake was replaced by internal brakes

172
00:13:12,021 --> 00:13:15,980
that relied on steel cables and pulleys

173
00:13:15,981 --> 00:13:17,460
and the strength of the driver

174
00:13:17,461 --> 00:13:19,720
to provide enough force to stop the car

175
00:13:19,721 --> 00:13:27,000
Strong steel cables provide powerful, unfailing and flexible connections between the brake

176
00:13:27,001 --> 00:13:28,500
pedal and the brake itself.

177
00:13:28,501 --> 00:13:32,440
Of course, as the weight of the vehicles increased and their speed increased, we had

178
00:13:32,441 --> 00:13:36,160
to come up with more effective ways to stop these machines.

179
00:13:36,161 --> 00:13:41,480
In 1918, aviation pioneer Malcolm Lockheed began to develop a hydraulic brake system

180
00:13:41,481 --> 00:13:43,860
for automobiles.

181
00:13:43,861 --> 00:13:48,680
Using petroleum oil as a lubricant, Lockheed designed a hydraulic system that transmitted

182
00:13:48,681 --> 00:13:53,420
fluid pressure against the brake drum allowing for smoother improved braking

183
00:13:53,421 --> 00:14:01,800
ability in 1920 Lockheed sold his brake patent to Vincent Bendix founder of the

184
00:14:01,801 --> 00:14:08,300
Bendix Corporation a year later the Duesenberg became the first passenger

185
00:14:08,301 --> 00:14:13,280
car to come equipped with a four-wheel hydraulic brake system the way that the

186
00:14:13,281 --> 00:14:18,380
early hydraulic systems for braking worked on cars was your pedal would push

187
00:14:18,381 --> 00:14:23,840
on a piston in a cylinder full of hydraulic fluid and that would push the

188
00:14:23,841 --> 00:14:27,260
fluid to each wheel. The fluid would come into another cylinder with two little

189
00:14:27,261 --> 00:14:32,600
pistons that push out against a couple of shoes, sort of curved shoes that push

190
00:14:32,601 --> 00:14:35,940
out onto a cylindrical drum and when they press on it like that it creates

191
00:14:35,941 --> 00:14:40,520
friction and slows the car down. So suddenly you can transmit a lot of energy to

192
00:14:40,521 --> 00:14:46,280
these individual wheel brakes. Although the brakes were a success, the rest of

193
00:14:46,281 --> 00:14:49,320
the automotive industry was slow to adapt.

194
00:14:49,321 --> 00:14:53,460
The American motorist was comfortable with mechanical brakes.

195
00:14:53,461 --> 00:14:57,160
You can see, here's a cable, here's the link, it's right out where I can see it.

196
00:14:57,161 --> 00:15:02,440
And to try and convince this person that you can push fluid through a line, whoa, that

197
00:15:02,441 --> 00:15:03,840
was kind of a mind blower.

198
00:15:03,841 --> 00:15:09,440
So it was a difficult uphill battle to make that case, and they had to sort of do it by

199
00:15:09,441 --> 00:15:13,640
just demonstrating how much more effective it was.

200
00:15:13,641 --> 00:15:18,720
hydraulic application that the automotive industry was hoping consumers would soon demand was power

201
00:15:18,721 --> 00:15:19,421
steering.

202
00:15:19,422 --> 00:15:24,340
But the Great Depression of the 1930s brought its development to a screeching halt.

203
00:15:25,860 --> 00:15:30,300
Poverty-stricken Americans didn't have the means or the interest to spend money on the latest

204
00:15:30,301 --> 00:15:32,512
automobile gadget to hit the market.

205
00:15:32,513 --> 00:15:36,260
By the time the American economy began to reinvigorate itself

206
00:15:36,261 --> 00:15:41,020
in the 1940s, another crippling event was to take hold of America.

207
00:15:43,640 --> 00:15:50,160
Most of the R&D efforts for improving hydraulics went into the war effort for tanks and vehicles and ships and things.

208
00:15:51,280 --> 00:15:55,780
And so really after World War II was over and people who had been in the war got back into the workplace,

209
00:15:56,040 --> 00:15:58,920
that saw a real blossoming of the hydraulic industry.

210
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:02,360
The automobile industry slipped into high gear.

211
00:16:03,160 --> 00:16:07,480
Drivers were ready to go faster than ever before, and Detroit was happy to oblige them.

212
00:16:08,340 --> 00:16:10,260
Those engines were made out of cast iron.

213
00:16:10,261 --> 00:16:14,260
They were big and heavy, and they were almost exclusively in the front.

214
00:16:14,261 --> 00:16:16,260
They were over the steering wheels.

215
00:16:16,261 --> 00:16:21,260
So with all that weight, it became very difficult to turn those wheels.

216
00:16:21,261 --> 00:16:27,260
The technology for power steering had been developed for the automobile before World War II,

217
00:16:27,261 --> 00:16:31,260
making it an easy transition for auto manufacturers.

218
00:16:31,261 --> 00:16:35,260
Just take a hydraulic pump and have this driven by the engine,

219
00:16:35,261 --> 00:16:41,240
And the combination of the hydraulic flow and pressure is what provided the power to turn the wheels to the left or right.

220
00:16:41,460 --> 00:16:44,500
If you turn it to the left, all it does is actuate a valve.

221
00:16:44,860 --> 00:16:49,680
The valve opens and routes fluid into a piston, which would turn the wheel to the left.

222
00:16:50,640 --> 00:16:52,920
So power steering became an absolute godsend.

223
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,660
And the early ones, they wanted to make themselves known.

224
00:16:56,800 --> 00:16:59,880
And so your power steering would be totally zero effort.

225
00:17:00,040 --> 00:17:03,320
I mean, you could, with your pinky finger, steer these gigantic cars.

226
00:17:05,260 --> 00:17:10,820
Since the advent of power steering and brakes, their hydraulic systems have largely remained the same.

227
00:17:12,040 --> 00:17:19,300
Brakes are now complemented with vacuum assist, making it possible to stop even the largest SUV with just a touch from your foot.

228
00:17:20,360 --> 00:17:27,360
Power steering also features power assist, which increases the flow of hydraulic fluid based on the speed with which the steering wheel is turned.

229
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:34,620
Modern hydraulic applications for the automobile focus on style and driver comfort.

230
00:17:35,260 --> 00:17:44,980
You can spend a car with metal springs, but you can also put a piston there with hydraulics to get the same type of springing effect.

231
00:17:45,960 --> 00:17:54,940
The holy grail of suspension is active body control, a feature only found in high-end luxury cars like the Mercedes-Benz CL Coupe.

232
00:17:56,120 --> 00:18:02,340
Active body control will keep the car steady and level, even on the sharpest turns or the bumpiest of roads.

233
00:18:02,341 --> 00:18:07,360
the system works by placing a hydraulic plunger on top of the suspension coil.

234
00:18:08,920 --> 00:18:12,140
If the car runs over a pothole, within milliseconds,

235
00:18:12,940 --> 00:18:15,860
sensors located throughout the car activate the plunger,

236
00:18:16,520 --> 00:18:19,120
causing it to extend and balance the car body.

237
00:18:20,180 --> 00:18:24,100
But perhaps its most unique feature is the ability to change suspension.

238
00:18:25,400 --> 00:18:27,180
Want the feel of a high-end sports car?

239
00:18:27,181 --> 00:18:30,320
Just push a button and the hydraulics will make it happen.

240
00:18:32,340 --> 00:18:38,340
Although the invention of hydraulic brakes and power steering helped drive the automotive business,

241
00:18:38,341 --> 00:18:45,340
the impact these innovations had on the construction industry literally changed the face of the earth.

242
00:18:48,340 --> 00:18:53,340
Vincent Bendix, founder of Bendix Brakes, was inspired to develop a better car brake

243
00:18:53,341 --> 00:18:58,340
after his father was hit and killed by a car with defective cable brakes.

244
00:19:02,340 --> 00:19:11,560
The greatest example of the brute strength that hydraulics can deliver is found in the construction industry.

245
00:19:18,460 --> 00:19:26,100
Over the years, the excavators, cranes, and trucks that have built our world have grown exponentially in size.

246
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:32,240
Achieving unmatched efficiency and power through hydraulics.

247
00:19:32,340 --> 00:19:36,680
If you go to a construction site, you can't help but see hydraulics.

248
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:40,640
It's everywhere. It's in virtually every machine that's on the construction site.

249
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:46,080
And in many cases, or probably most cases, it's because there's just no better way of doing it.

250
00:19:48,040 --> 00:19:50,260
At a warehouse in Youngstown, Ohio,

251
00:19:50,980 --> 00:19:55,420
the Robbins Company is putting the final touches on two gigantic tunnel boring machines.

252
00:19:57,040 --> 00:20:01,820
The drills are nearly 400 feet long and weigh a hefty 800 tons.

253
00:20:02,340 --> 00:20:07,400
To move something this massive, there was only one practical alternative.

254
00:20:08,780 --> 00:20:16,300
The amount of force we get from the hydraulics far exceeds what we can get from electrics without going into great cost.

255
00:20:16,920 --> 00:20:21,880
Hydraulic gives you more force and power, especially in these type of machines.

256
00:20:22,480 --> 00:20:25,680
You need that force to cut the rock as hard as it can be sometimes.

257
00:20:25,681 --> 00:20:34,020
When the drill is activated, 800 gallons of hydraulic fluid travel through seven miles

258
00:20:34,021 --> 00:20:40,000
of hosing to hundreds of hydraulic components.

259
00:20:40,001 --> 00:20:46,300
The hydraulic system is capable of pushing the drill's pistons at a force of 5,250 pounds

260
00:20:46,301 --> 00:20:48,240
of pressure per square inch.

261
00:20:48,241 --> 00:20:54,960
When we talk about hydraulic pressure, what we're doing is putting force into a cylinder

262
00:20:54,961 --> 00:20:58,540
which in turn puts force against a given component.

263
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:03,240
Doing the cutting is a 24-foot diameter drill head

264
00:21:03,241 --> 00:21:06,840
that runs on 10, 450-horsepower motors.

265
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:10,700
The drill head features 46 19-inch teeth

266
00:21:10,701 --> 00:21:13,900
that will shatter rock at a rate of 25 feet an hour.

267
00:21:14,660 --> 00:21:16,460
Although electrics power the drill head,

268
00:21:16,820 --> 00:21:19,180
it's the hydraulics that makes the cutting possible.

269
00:21:20,160 --> 00:21:23,860
Behind me you see some curved items we call a gripper shoe.

270
00:21:23,861 --> 00:21:30,760
So if we put 5,000 pounds of pressure on that shoe, that is giving us enough force to hold

271
00:21:30,761 --> 00:21:35,580
that machine in place so that it doesn't try to spin in the tunnel.

272
00:21:35,581 --> 00:21:40,100
The grip shoe transmits 4 million pounds of force against a tunnel wall.

273
00:21:40,101 --> 00:21:44,820
When the drill is stabilized, a second set of cylinders extend, pushing the drill forward

274
00:21:44,821 --> 00:21:47,960
at a force of 3 million pounds.

275
00:21:47,961 --> 00:21:49,960
That shoe weighs about 40,000 pounds.

276
00:21:49,961 --> 00:21:58,360
And as it moves very smoothly, as you see, it just shows you how the hydraulics move that kind of weight smoothly and steady.

277
00:21:59,500 --> 00:22:05,700
After inspection, the mammoth drills will be sent to Iceland for the construction of a hydroelectric power plant.

278
00:22:07,820 --> 00:22:14,820
Prodigious construction equipment, such as the Robins tunnel boring machines, represents the unparalleled power that hydraulics can offer.

279
00:22:14,821 --> 00:22:23,580
but the history of hydraulics in construction had a fairly modest beginning starting with a

280
00:22:23,581 --> 00:22:31,980
flatbed truck by 1910 the truck was altered from a simple transportation vehicle to a machine that

281
00:22:31,981 --> 00:22:40,500
could carry large bulk materials such as dirt rock and coal placing these materials into the

282
00:22:40,501 --> 00:22:44,500
The truck bed was the job of the steam shovel.

283
00:22:44,501 --> 00:22:47,500
Unloading them was done with a mechanical dump body.

284
00:22:47,501 --> 00:22:52,500
The construction industry today relies on the hydraulic cylinder.

285
00:22:52,501 --> 00:22:58,500
If you consider the dump truck, the early trucks consisted of a winch and pulley system.

286
00:22:58,501 --> 00:23:05,500
The operator had to get out of the cab, wind up this crank to dump the bed of the truck.

287
00:23:05,501 --> 00:23:15,080
Today, they sit in the cab, they push a button, the hydraulics extends a ram from the cylinder and empties the bed of the truck.

288
00:23:15,860 --> 00:23:26,680
The hydraulic cylinder is a method of converting the hydraulic energy to a workable energy in a back-and-forth motion.

289
00:23:26,800 --> 00:23:29,520
And that's really all it does is it moves in or it moves out.

290
00:23:29,521 --> 00:23:36,140
This is the popular hydraulic type, which operates easily and efficiently under all loads.

291
00:23:36,780 --> 00:23:39,880
The end gate is double-acting and is operated by a control level.

292
00:23:41,560 --> 00:23:47,640
The increased efficiency and reliability of the hydraulic dump truck resonated throughout the construction industry.

293
00:23:48,440 --> 00:23:54,360
But the impact of the Great Depression and World War II stifled the growth of hydraulic construction machines.

294
00:23:54,361 --> 00:24:00,660
After the war, new hydraulic pumps that were designed to increase fluid pressure for tanks

295
00:24:00,661 --> 00:24:04,560
and aircraft were introduced to the revitalized construction industry.

296
00:24:06,520 --> 00:24:11,400
Coupled with the advent of power steering and hydraulic brakes, manufacturers started

297
00:24:11,401 --> 00:24:15,760
to build bigger, heavier, and more powerful machines than ever before.

298
00:24:16,860 --> 00:24:20,860
The construction industry has to move great quantities of earth.

299
00:24:21,380 --> 00:24:26,080
So you could have 10 backhoes loading 10 trucks,

300
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:31,520
or you could have one backhoe loading one large truck.

301
00:24:32,080 --> 00:24:35,860
And when you did that, you had to move the same amount of earth,

302
00:24:36,040 --> 00:24:39,500
so that means the piece of equipment had to be much larger.

303
00:24:41,440 --> 00:24:45,620
Today, construction vehicles are some of the largest machines in the world,

304
00:24:45,621 --> 00:24:48,300
with nearly all of them utilizing hydraulics,

305
00:24:48,301 --> 00:24:52,920
including the Caterpillar 330CL excavator.

306
00:24:53,520 --> 00:24:56,540
The weight of this arm is in the neighborhood of 10,000 pounds.

307
00:24:57,980 --> 00:25:03,520
We'd have quite a bit of problem moving that arm efficiently without the use of hydraulics.

308
00:25:07,680 --> 00:25:12,480
Hydraulics also makes it possible to maneuver the Liebherr T-282B mining truck

309
00:25:12,481 --> 00:25:18,440
and its 400-ton payload capacity?

310
00:25:19,960 --> 00:25:22,120
Well, hydraulic cylinders provide the power

311
00:25:22,121 --> 00:25:24,460
to lift the bed nearly 50 feet into the air.

312
00:25:28,400 --> 00:25:30,380
Cranes rely on hydraulics, too.

313
00:25:31,260 --> 00:25:33,120
Taylor Machine Works' 80-foot-long

314
00:25:33,121 --> 00:25:35,940
and 80-foot-wide gantry crane called Big Red

315
00:25:35,941 --> 00:25:41,660
uses hydraulics to lift cargo containers

316
00:25:41,661 --> 00:25:44,440
that weigh upwards of 90,000 pounds.

317
00:25:45,580 --> 00:25:47,560
But hydraulic construction machines

318
00:25:47,561 --> 00:25:49,760
don't always focus on strength and power.

319
00:25:51,420 --> 00:25:53,980
Timberjack's tree forester uses hydraulics

320
00:25:53,981 --> 00:25:56,400
to walk through the forest with the grace of a dancer.

321
00:26:00,240 --> 00:26:03,280
But you might want to watch your toes around this ballerina.

322
00:26:03,920 --> 00:26:09,740
Whether it's walking, dumping, digging, or drilling,

323
00:26:09,741 --> 00:26:14,280
the environment of a construction site is one of the dirtiest places on earth

324
00:26:14,281 --> 00:26:20,980
yet the most identifiable component of the hydraulic machine the cylinder never appears

325
00:26:20,981 --> 00:26:25,740
to lose its luster any kind of contamination within the hydraulic system is going to have

326
00:26:25,741 --> 00:26:33,500
a devastating effect upon the system itself the cylinders have a wiper on the end of the cylinder

327
00:26:33,501 --> 00:26:39,620
head that as the cylinder rod retracts it cleans off the excess dirt so when it extends again

328
00:26:39,621 --> 00:26:41,220
and it's as though it were needed.

329
00:26:43,400 --> 00:26:46,800
But hydraulics is not just revolutionized machines on the ground.

330
00:26:47,380 --> 00:26:49,940
It's revolutionized machines in the sky, too.

331
00:26:53,300 --> 00:26:58,720
From the latest military planes to the future of commercial air travel.

332
00:27:00,720 --> 00:27:06,280
Speed boat racing pioneer Garfield Wood invented the hydraulic lift in 1911

333
00:27:06,281 --> 00:27:10,240
as a means to unload coal from truck beds more efficiently.

334
00:27:17,080 --> 00:27:25,160
Over the last 100 years, the aerospace industry has transformed from this to this.

335
00:27:26,500 --> 00:27:32,840
The astounding leap in aircraft technology would not have been possible without the power of hydraulics.

336
00:27:32,841 --> 00:27:36,840
The airplane really shows a prime example of one of the advantages of hydraulics,

337
00:27:36,841 --> 00:27:40,840
to where you can have a single place where you generate your power through the engine

338
00:27:40,841 --> 00:27:44,840
and transmit this through hydraulic lines to wherever you need it,

339
00:27:44,841 --> 00:27:46,840
whether it's at the back of the plane or in the wings.

340
00:27:50,840 --> 00:27:55,840
Inside the body of a passenger aircraft is an intricate system of pumps and hoses

341
00:27:55,841 --> 00:28:00,840
that transfers the highly pressurized fluid that commands nearly every phase of flight control.

342
00:28:02,840 --> 00:28:06,760
Most of these systems operate by what is known as fly-by-wire.

343
00:28:07,960 --> 00:28:14,840
In a typical fly-by-wire system, flight command is controlled electrically, but powered hydraulically.

344
00:28:17,180 --> 00:28:21,400
When a pilot wants to move the rudder, the command is input into a computer.

345
00:28:21,960 --> 00:28:25,320
The signal is then sent electrically by wire to the hydraulic system.

346
00:28:26,800 --> 00:28:32,660
This system consists of one reservoir per engine that supplies fluid to two pumps per engine.

347
00:28:32,840 --> 00:28:39,600
The pumps send fluid pressurized at 3,000 pounds per square inch through metal tubing

348
00:28:39,601 --> 00:28:44,120
that extends the remaining length of the fuselage where it connects to an actuator, pushing

349
00:28:44,121 --> 00:28:49,360
a piston and moving the rudder.

350
00:28:49,361 --> 00:28:53,840
This powerful and efficient system of fluid pressure transference is a huge contrast to

351
00:28:53,841 --> 00:28:59,640
the one used by Orville and Wilbur Wright over a hundred years ago.

352
00:28:59,641 --> 00:29:02,260
The Wright Brothers aircraft had no hydraulics on it.

353
00:29:02,880 --> 00:29:07,440
Its basic control was by a lever and cables or pulleys,

354
00:29:07,600 --> 00:29:10,480
and this allowed them to have the three axes of control.

355
00:29:11,980 --> 00:29:16,040
During World War I, the slow-moving dual-wing airplanes

356
00:29:16,041 --> 00:29:19,300
functioned similarly to early Wright Brothers flyers,

357
00:29:20,020 --> 00:29:24,100
relying on the strength of the aviator to move cables and pulleys for flight control.

358
00:29:24,980 --> 00:29:29,360
Those were small aircraft, so therefore it was very easy for the pilot

359
00:29:29,361 --> 00:29:35,600
to control the motion of the actuators simply by moving the stick back and forth.

360
00:29:37,080 --> 00:29:42,700
After World War I, the military looked at ways to increase the speed and performance of the airplane.

361
00:29:44,900 --> 00:29:47,800
Engineers focused their attention on the landing gear.

362
00:29:48,760 --> 00:29:50,520
All landing gear before were fixed.

363
00:29:51,060 --> 00:29:58,200
The Navy decided to do some test in which they actually raised the landing gear through a crank and pulley arrangement,

364
00:29:58,201 --> 00:30:02,200
and they found that it could reduce to drag about 30 percent,

365
00:30:02,600 --> 00:30:06,580
but it was very intensive and therefore hydraulics was looked at

366
00:30:06,581 --> 00:30:09,180
as a way of doing this very effectively.

367
00:30:09,780 --> 00:30:15,080
The first hydraulically operated landing gear system was produced in 1929,

368
00:30:15,980 --> 00:30:18,780
opening the door for hydraulic applications on aircraft.

369
00:30:19,780 --> 00:30:23,920
In the years that followed, planes continued to grow in size and speed,

370
00:30:23,921 --> 00:30:29,440
increasing pilot difficulty to command the aircraft using the stick and pulley system.

371
00:30:30,440 --> 00:30:34,300
To be able to move the elevators, for example, or the rudder or the air lines.

372
00:30:34,580 --> 00:30:39,120
Trying to do it by hand or the conventional pulley and cable system,

373
00:30:39,121 --> 00:30:41,860
most of those aircraft would be into the ground or the water.

374
00:30:42,920 --> 00:30:45,880
With the success of hydraulically operated landing gear,

375
00:30:46,540 --> 00:30:49,860
military engineers followed the lead set by the automobile industry,

376
00:30:49,861 --> 00:30:54,000
installing hydraulic brakes aboard all military aircraft.

377
00:30:55,740 --> 00:30:57,640
Soon everything from flaps and rudders

378
00:30:57,641 --> 00:31:00,680
to gun turrets and bomb bay doors

379
00:31:00,681 --> 00:31:02,880
were utilizing hydraulics to actuate.

380
00:31:03,880 --> 00:31:06,940
Although the flight control system still required the pilot

381
00:31:06,941 --> 00:31:08,420
to move cables and pulleys,

382
00:31:09,080 --> 00:31:12,500
the actuators now moved with hydraulic multiplication of force.

383
00:31:13,860 --> 00:31:17,200
But the planes of World War II needed to perform maneuvers

384
00:31:17,201 --> 00:31:19,600
faster and swifter than ever before.

385
00:31:19,860 --> 00:31:28,360
The pressures have to increase to be able to allow the pilot to move the surfaces, to do what he wanted to do.

386
00:31:29,440 --> 00:31:31,120
You needed a new type of pump.

387
00:31:32,980 --> 00:31:39,640
The gear pumps, which were being used on most military aircraft, were inefficient and would leak at high pressures.

388
00:31:41,320 --> 00:31:48,460
Hydraulic pioneer Harry Vickers designed a piston pump, which quickly became the predominant pump for World War II aircraft.

389
00:31:49,860 --> 00:31:56,840
With these new piston pumps, fluid pressure increased from 1,000 psi to 3,000 psi.

390
00:31:59,200 --> 00:32:04,160
But this increase in pressure also added stress to the hydraulic hoses and sealants.

391
00:32:05,800 --> 00:32:12,940
If a hydraulic fluid would spray onto an engine manifold, it would certainly cause a fire.

392
00:32:12,941 --> 00:32:19,140
And many of the fires that you've seen, in general, those were hydraulic leaks that started the fires.

393
00:32:19,860 --> 00:32:23,440
Many times a hydraulic leak would not be something you'd think of with dripping.

394
00:32:23,760 --> 00:32:26,200
It's high pressure, so it would leak out of a hose or a fitting.

395
00:32:26,560 --> 00:32:28,560
It would often come out as a spray.

396
00:32:28,980 --> 00:32:32,080
If there was a fire already started, the leaking oil would feed the fire

397
00:32:32,081 --> 00:32:33,420
and you'd have catastrophic events.

398
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:40,040
To combat this, the military changed to a less flammable kerosene oil

399
00:32:40,041 --> 00:32:42,420
called Mil H 5606.

400
00:32:43,440 --> 00:32:47,100
A new sealant was designed as well, known simply as the O-ring.

401
00:32:48,040 --> 00:32:53,780
The O-ring has a unique ability to be able to compress, to fill a space,

402
00:32:53,940 --> 00:32:56,940
and it's able to do that very efficiently.

403
00:32:57,380 --> 00:33:00,020
It's a donut-shaped piece of elastomer,

404
00:33:00,540 --> 00:33:07,400
and it became the seal of choice for most of the products that are in a hydraulic system.

405
00:33:08,280 --> 00:33:12,460
By the end of World War II, innovations in aeronautic hydraulics

406
00:33:12,461 --> 00:33:16,000
found an easy transition into the burgeoning commercial aircraft industry.

407
00:33:17,100 --> 00:33:21,060
Commercial airlines discovered that they could also take advantage of the hydraulics

408
00:33:21,061 --> 00:33:25,020
to where their aircraft could be made larger, lighter, and able to hold more people.

409
00:33:25,300 --> 00:33:30,480
So by having these aircraft that could carry more people, it made airline travel more practical.

410
00:33:31,960 --> 00:33:38,440
The mil-aged kerosene-based fluids used in military aircraft were deemed unsafe by the FAA.

411
00:33:39,520 --> 00:33:46,020
In 1948, aircraft manufacturers addressed this concern with the development of Skydrol 7000.

412
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:50,300
This fluid was a phosphate ester and is still in use today.

413
00:33:50,840 --> 00:33:54,020
Phosphate esters are a class of synthetic hydraulic fluids

414
00:33:54,021 --> 00:33:57,120
which have very good lubricating qualities.

415
00:33:57,660 --> 00:34:00,340
In addition, they tend to be fire resistant.

416
00:34:02,380 --> 00:34:03,700
Except for the fluid,

417
00:34:04,380 --> 00:34:06,840
the hydraulic functions of early commercial aircraft

418
00:34:06,841 --> 00:34:09,720
were almost identical to those used on military planes.

419
00:34:10,980 --> 00:34:12,820
Hydraulics actuated the landing gear,

420
00:34:13,440 --> 00:34:14,660
moved flaps and rudders,

421
00:34:14,661 --> 00:34:17,060
and controlled the brakes.

422
00:34:17,860 --> 00:34:20,600
Because of the size and weight of commercial airliners,

423
00:34:21,440 --> 00:34:24,580
hydraulic pressure increased to 3,000 PSI,

424
00:34:25,240 --> 00:34:28,260
the same pressure already used on most military aircraft

425
00:34:28,261 --> 00:34:29,960
and construction machines.

426
00:34:31,540 --> 00:34:34,820
Although this increase in fluid pressure gave pilots the muscle

427
00:34:34,821 --> 00:34:37,160
to operate actuators through cables and pulleys,

428
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:40,820
by the 1980s, it was time for a change.

429
00:34:42,100 --> 00:34:44,400
The problem with the cable and pulley is

430
00:34:44,401 --> 00:34:47,100
as surfaces become larger and larger,

431
00:34:47,300 --> 00:34:50,040
it is very difficult even to give the correct feel.

432
00:34:50,540 --> 00:34:52,840
Electronics can make this decision much quicker.

433
00:34:53,200 --> 00:34:56,320
Today's aircraft have much greater performance capabilities

434
00:34:56,321 --> 00:34:59,960
and have to do things like moving these surfaces almost instantaneously.

435
00:35:01,020 --> 00:35:04,980
Fly-by-wire hydraulic systems continue to dominate the flight control

436
00:35:04,981 --> 00:35:08,120
of even the most advanced commercial and military aircraft.

437
00:35:09,020 --> 00:35:13,460
The hydraulics aboard the F-22 fighter plane are pretty much state-of-the-art.

438
00:35:13,461 --> 00:35:22,920
We have all of the primary flight controls, the landing gear, the weapons bay doors open very quickly and close very quickly to launch missiles.

439
00:35:23,280 --> 00:35:30,320
The propulsion system depends very heavily on hydraulics in the F-22 for control of the inlets and the exhaust gnaws.

440
00:35:32,960 --> 00:35:40,860
The biggest advancement in hydraulic technology for aircraft is the development of the EHA, or Electric Hydrostatic Actuator.

441
00:35:40,861 --> 00:35:50,820
Originally developed in the early 90s, the EHA contains all the components of a hydraulic system in one small package.

442
00:35:52,280 --> 00:36:01,680
By placing EHAs next to aircraft control surfaces, designers eliminate thousands of feet of hydraulic tubing and hundreds of pounds of weight.

443
00:36:02,340 --> 00:36:10,600
This EHA is considered cutting-edge technology because it removes the distribution elements that are part of a conventional hydraulic system.

444
00:36:10,601 --> 00:36:13,600
There are two electric motors that work in tandem.

445
00:36:14,080 --> 00:36:21,020
We also have hydraulic reservoirs to capture the thermal expansion contraction required to power the hydraulic pumps of this system.

446
00:36:21,420 --> 00:36:25,980
The linkage here is attached directly to the flight control surface.

447
00:36:27,580 --> 00:36:35,060
The first aircraft to use EHA technology for primary flight control will be the F-35 Joint Strike Fighter.

448
00:36:40,600 --> 00:36:47,600
The first commercial aircraft to feature EHAs, in this case for secondary flight control,

449
00:36:47,601 --> 00:36:50,600
will be the new Airbus A380.

450
00:36:50,601 --> 00:36:57,600
Boasting a length of 239 feet and a wingspan nearly as long as a football field,

451
00:36:57,601 --> 00:37:01,600
this is the largest commercial airplane ever to be built.

452
00:37:01,601 --> 00:37:08,600
New hydraulic technology had to be created to control an aircraft that weighs well over 300 tons.

453
00:37:08,601 --> 00:37:15,760
Certainly the A380 offered the hydraulic system designer several major challenges.

454
00:37:15,761 --> 00:37:29,700
If we were going to use the conventional hydraulic system concepts, 3000 PSI for example, the pumps would be massive, the lines, fittings and hoses would be massive.

455
00:37:29,980 --> 00:37:38,080
A solution to this was to increase the pressure, not from 3000 to 4000, but to take it to the next step, 5000 PSI.

456
00:37:38,600 --> 00:37:46,600
But as the aerospace industry continues to look for ways to improve upon the efficiency and safety of flight control systems,

457
00:37:46,601 --> 00:37:53,600
engineers are investigating the idea of using electric motors to actuate control surfaces,

458
00:37:53,601 --> 00:37:56,600
thus eliminating hydraulics completely from flight control.

459
00:37:58,600 --> 00:38:02,600
Before that happens, a formidable hurdle lies ahead.

460
00:38:02,601 --> 00:38:06,740
engineers need to develop a practical motor that can create the needed force

461
00:38:06,741 --> 00:38:11,160
to actuate the surface controls efficiently.

462
00:38:11,660 --> 00:38:17,220
But hydraulics not only shapes our future, it brings the past to life. Prehistoric

463
00:38:17,221 --> 00:38:23,300
dinosaurs animate with the help of hydraulics. The Airbus A380 will feature

464
00:38:23,301 --> 00:38:28,880
four independent primary flight control systems. Each of these systems is capable

465
00:38:28,881 --> 00:38:34,080
of operating the aircraft should the others fail, creating a redundancy system unequaled

466
00:38:34,081 --> 00:38:36,260
by any military or commercial plane.

467
00:38:43,860 --> 00:38:48,920
Hydraulics not only plays a key role in our work lives, it plays a role in our play lives, too.

468
00:38:51,540 --> 00:38:56,340
Universal Studios in Hollywood, California is home to some of the most cutting-edge hydraulic

469
00:38:56,341 --> 00:38:57,500
attractions in the world.

470
00:38:58,880 --> 00:39:05,412
You would not be able to have any of these attractions move this much weight and be this reliable without hydraulics.

471
00:39:05,414 --> 00:39:06,920
It just would never happen.

472
00:39:07,920 --> 00:39:13,160
One of the more complex hydraulic attractions at Universal Studios is Earthquake the Ride.

473
00:39:15,060 --> 00:39:20,500
Passengers entering this stage are given an idea of what it's like to endure a massive seismic event.

474
00:39:28,880 --> 00:39:42,880
The hydraulic system for Earthquake runs on just two 50-horsepower motors.

475
00:39:44,640 --> 00:39:49,300
The system works by sending fluid through hydraulic pumps to a series of accumulators

476
00:39:49,301 --> 00:39:55,360
that store the fluid at pressure, keeping it ready for each show.

477
00:39:55,361 --> 00:40:00,360
Underneath the floor of earthquake, a dark cavern is home to dozens of hydraulic cylinders.

478
00:40:00,361 --> 00:40:06,900
Moving the 20,000-pound floor bed in linear and lateral movements, the cylinders create

479
00:40:06,901 --> 00:40:10,180
the feeling of an 8.3 temblor.

480
00:40:10,181 --> 00:40:13,840
But that's only one of the hydraulic effects on this ride.

481
00:40:13,841 --> 00:40:17,420
As the show's going on, we have a large slab that'll fall.

482
00:40:17,421 --> 00:40:20,920
It's got four big hydraulic cylinders behind it.

483
00:40:20,921 --> 00:40:23,820
It is probably somewhere around 9,000 pounds.

484
00:40:25,400 --> 00:40:29,540
And also we have a train that comes out and crashes right in front of the tram.

485
00:40:29,880 --> 00:40:31,700
It's driven out by a hydraulic ram.

486
00:40:33,520 --> 00:40:37,200
When the tram exits the stage, the hydraulic cylinders retract,

487
00:40:37,860 --> 00:40:40,480
carefully putting all the props back to their original positions.

488
00:40:41,760 --> 00:40:45,260
You have to keep the show exactly the same every single time.

489
00:40:47,360 --> 00:40:49,200
Earthquakes, hydraulics are very sophisticated.

490
00:40:49,201 --> 00:40:54,200
Universal's Terminator 2 3D also features several precision

491
00:40:54,201 --> 00:40:59,200
hydraulic effects. Hydraulics control the platforms that raise

492
00:40:59,201 --> 00:41:04,200
and lower different characters onto the stage. And they also

493
00:41:04,201 --> 00:41:09,200
animate the six gun-toting Cyberdyne robots using 3,000 PSI

494
00:41:09,201 --> 00:41:15,200
pumps to actuate cylinders in the head, torso, and arms.

495
00:41:19,200 --> 00:41:27,180
But the attraction that utilizes the most hydraulic effects at Universal is Jurassic Park,

496
00:41:27,181 --> 00:41:27,881
the ride.

497
00:41:27,882 --> 00:41:33,140
Visitors cruise through prehistoric tropics, encountering a bevy of life-size and

498
00:41:33,141 --> 00:41:40,260
lifelike dinosaurs, including a 40-foot tall ultrasaur that uses 15 hydraulic cylinders

499
00:41:40,261 --> 00:41:41,520
in the neck alone.

500
00:41:41,521 --> 00:41:50,960
This particular figure we just saw, the oil that comes in here, this is a pressure line.

501
00:41:50,961 --> 00:41:53,400
It's set at 3,000 psi.

502
00:41:53,401 --> 00:41:59,020
The hydraulic module here will distribute the oil to the figure itself, which has the

503
00:41:59,021 --> 00:42:01,020
one we saw, it's just a torso and head.

504
00:42:01,021 --> 00:42:06,800
It has six separate moves, six separate cylinders, and it's all powered by hydraulic.

505
00:42:06,801 --> 00:42:11,120
After encountering several dinosaurs, the ride drops another hydraulic throw, so to

506
00:42:11,121 --> 00:42:16,155
speak. We use hydraulics on the following car for two reasons really.

507
00:42:16,156 --> 00:42:17,760
It's more controllable

508
00:42:17,761 --> 00:42:21,811
than any other kind of power and it's also less expensive energy-wise.

509
00:42:21,812 --> 00:42:23,200
The cylinder is actually

510
00:42:23,201 --> 00:42:25,333
hooked up to a block and tackle.

511
00:42:25,334 --> 00:42:29,600
What that does is that increases the motion of the Jeep compared

512
00:42:29,601 --> 00:42:34,255
to the cylinder. The Jeep will move 32 feet while the cylinder will only move nine.

513
00:42:34,256 --> 00:42:35,040
So all we need

514
00:42:35,041 --> 00:42:37,800
a nine-foot cylinder to get that much movement.

515
00:42:37,801 --> 00:42:40,480
That increases the force on the cylinder,

516
00:42:40,481 --> 00:42:42,580
but the hydraulics overcomes that,

517
00:42:42,581 --> 00:42:44,720
and it's no problem at all.

518
00:42:44,721 --> 00:42:47,420
Then there's Waterworld,

519
00:42:47,421 --> 00:42:49,120
which uses a hydraulic catapult

520
00:42:49,121 --> 00:42:51,160
to launch a 2,000-pound airplane

